#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2944a0c-49e4-504f-814b-58660cce1be4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = 2\\ln (1 + t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163545} {:answer/id #uuid "cab5b877-64cd-5318-8761-44d2a864729e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163546} {:answer/id #uuid "e99dc0fd-cb7f-580d-a040-9b89cb077f10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\ln (1 + t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163547} {:answer/id #uuid "3e4a0fbe-fbb0-5ed6-8577-c75c2555a5c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = 1 - e^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163548} {:answer/id #uuid "3fe0a7ef-2008-5e92-825c-5f5566c56429" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = e^{t} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fe0a7ef-2008-5e92-825c-5f5566c56429" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = e^{t} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163549} :answer-field/id #uuid "d106f175-8f9b-5a2d-a640-2633c9b956c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8488fc53-7024-5b0e-acc8-a7217b9f9e03" :question/math-academy-id "q-122170" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)=(1+x)\\,m/s.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/assets/a8a6f49ead700bee856b1bbdbb10848eac0a57609da14e3967916a36eb56f048.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=1+x.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1+x.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1+x \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+x| & =t+K \\\\ 1+x & =C{e}^{t} \\\\ x & =C{e}^{t}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{t}-1.\n$$"}]}
